// shape: the BODY-WALKER GIVE-UP shapes — page bodies that validate clean and
// render NOTHING, so the only thing standing between the author and a blank
// screen is what the walker says about it (M-T9.55).
//
// Every construct below is valid `.ddd`.  `ddd parse` reports `0 error(s), 0
// warning(s)` on this file — that is the point of it.  Each one reaches a
// give-up in the frontend body walker, and before M-T9.55's third slice each
// left only a sentence of emitter prose in a generated comment: findable (the
// `loom:unrendered` sentinel, #2843) but not explicable, with no code to look
// up and nothing for a census or a future `generate system` pass to group on.
//
//   Timeline { }                  no `of:`/positional entries
//                                 → loom.page-primitive-arg-missing
//   IdLink { }                    no `of:` aggregate ref
//                                 → loom.page-primitive-arg-missing
//   DestroyForm { }               `of:` present in neither form
//                                 → loom.page-primitive-arg-invalid
//   Icon { name: "…" }            a name outside the builtin glyph registry
//                                 → loom.page-primitive-arg-invalid
//   CreateForm { of: "Ghost" }    names an aggregate this ui cannot reach
//                                 → loom.page-ref-unreachable
//
// The `of:` STRING form on the last one is deliberate and is not a trick: the
// walker accepts a string there as well as a ref, and the string form is the
// one that survives scope resolution with a name no aggregate carries — which
// is exactly the "page binds something the deployable does not serve" shape,
// reachable in real projects by removing a context from a backend deployable
// and leaving the page behind.
//
// Two codes in the family are NOT witnessed here, for reasons worth writing
// down rather than papering over:
//
//   * `loom.page-primitive-target-gap` is per-FRONTEND (Timeline on feliz /
//     flutter, a pack with no renderer, the HEEx instance-op-form), so it
//     cannot fire on the react host this fixture declares.  The cross-target
//     sweep in `test/generator/_walker/walker-declines-with-a-code.test.ts`
//     covers it on all seven targets.
//   * `loom.page-expr-unrenderable` is the markup-position expression
//     backstop (`walker-core.ts`'s `default:` arm).  No authored `.ddd` shape
//     is known to reach it today; it is carried as a coded backstop rather
//     than claimed as a witnessed condition.
//
// WHY IT IS NOT IN `test/fixtures/corpus/`.  The corpus is a BACKEND feature
// matrix, and two of its own gates say so normatively: `clause-census.test.ts`
// asserts "the corpus fixtures still carry no `ui`" (retro §82 — the corpus
// compile legs can never reach a frontend feature no matter how many fixtures
// they gain), and `feature-doc-coverage.test.ts`'s FEATURE_DOCS admits only
// docs describing an authorable DOMAIN feature.  A frontend give-up fixture
// satisfies neither.  Both gates caught the first attempt to put it there,
// which is the gates working; it lives here instead, read directly by
// `test/generator/_walker/walker-give-up-corpus-shapes.test.ts`.
//
// A concrete `platform: node` backend (not the corpus's `__PLATFORM__` token)
// for the same reason: the backend deployable exists only to give the ui
// something to fail to bind to, and nothing here varies with it.
system WalkerGiveUpShapes {
  subdomain Work {
    context Ops {
      aggregate Job with crudish {
        name: string
        note: string?
      }
    }
  }

  ui Console {
    page Home {
      route: "/"
      title: "Give-up shapes"
      body: Stack {
        Heading { "Give-up shapes", level: 1 },
        Timeline { },
        IdLink { },
        DestroyForm { },
        Icon { name: "no-such-glyph-in-the-builtin-registry" },
        CreateForm { of: "Ghost" }
      }
    }
  }

  api OpsApi from Work
  storage primary { type: postgres }
  resource opsState { for: Ops, kind: state, use: primary }
  deployable d {
    platform: node
    contexts: [Ops]
    dataSources: [opsState]
    serves: OpsApi
    port: 4000
  }
  deployable web {
    platform: react
    targets: d
    ui: Console
    port: 3000
  }
}
